From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Vogelaar <revogelaar@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 02:06:31 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] ctrl: initialise the counts of a data model built from a
 network; bound the radio array

em_ctrl can crash (SIGSEGV) when an agent onboards for the first time. The
backtrace ends in dm_radio_t::operator= under dm_easy_mesh_t::operator=,
em_cmd_t::init, the em_cmd_bsta_cap_t constructor and
dm_easy_mesh_ctrl_t::analyze_bsta_cap_req, reading past the top of the stack.

analyze_bsta_cap_req() (and analyze_sta_link_metrics() and
analyze_unassoc_sta_metrics_query()) starts with `dm_easy_mesh_t dm = *this;`. The controller is a
dm_network_t, so this goes through dm_easy_mesh_t(const dm_network_t&), which
initialised only m_wifi_data: m_num_radios and the other counts held whatever
the stack held. em_cmd_t::init() then copied the model, and operator= copied
obj.m_num_radios radios into m_radio[EM_MAX_BANDS], however many that was. A
moderate value corrupts the heap behind the command; a large one reads off the
stack and crashes. Whether and when it crashes depends on the stack contents.

- dm_easy_mesh_t(const dm_network_t&) delegates to the default constructor,
  which zeroes the counts.
- operator= copies at most EM_MAX_BANDS radios.
- Every place that appends a radio checks the array first and logs what it
  drops: both Radio Basic Capability handlers (which dereferenced the NULL
  get_radio() returns for index EM_MAX_BANDS), dm_easy_mesh_list_t::put_radio,
  commit_config()'s new radio, the em_insert orchestration, the RadioList
  decode, the policy request and the radio-enable target.
- The policy request's guard compared against EM_MAX_RADIO_PER_AGENT (4) for an
  array of EM_MAX_BANDS (3); it now uses EM_MAX_BANDS.
